# SRG-APP-000315-WSR-000004  -  CAT II
# The web server must restrict inbound connections from nonsecure zones.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000315-WSR-000004
group:           V-206417
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-002314
control_rev5:    AC-17

contribution:    Splunk holds a network access control list on every listener it owns -
                 web.conf acceptFrom for Splunk Web, server.conf [httpServer] acceptFrom
                 for the management interface, and acceptFrom on the receiving input stanza
                 - each of which defaults to accepting connections from anywhere until the
                 organization's rules are written into it.

governs:         inputs.conf - Configures inputs, distributed inputs such as forwarders, and file system
                 monitoring.
governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] acceptFrom not_matches (^|[,[:space:]])\*([,[:space:]]|$)
check:           web.conf [settings] acceptFrom not_equals *
check:           server.conf [httpServer] acceptFrom not_equals *
check:           inputs.conf [splunktcp-ssl:<port>] acceptFrom non_empty
check:           inputs.conf [splunktcp-ssl:<port>] acceptFrom not_matches (^|[ ,])(\*|0\.0\.0\.0/0|::/0)([ ,]|$)
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          inputs.conf [splunktcp-ssl:<port>] acceptFrom https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.2-configuration-file-reference/inputs.conf
vendor:          server.conf [httpServer] acceptFrom https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] acceptFrom https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The organization, and for this benchmark the DoD, defines which zones are
                 nonsecure; the application stores the resulting ACL but does not determine
                 it.
external:        Perimeter protection devices in front of the instance carry the rest of
                 this restriction and are not application settings.
external:        An input that is not declared in inputs.conf cannot be constrained by this
                 criterion, and forwarder-side configuration lives on a separate host.

citation:        Only the inputs.conf stanza changes, and it has to. acceptFrom is not a
                 global inputs.conf setting: the specification defines it per input stanza,
                 and the receiving stanza the product STIG's own fix configures is
                 [splunktcp-ssl:<port>] (SPLK-CL-000290; inputs.conf.spec:876, 908-910,
                 with the rule syntax and "Default: * (accept from anywhere)" at 577-591).
                 A condition attached to an empty stanza name cannot be looked up in any
                 file, so it would report a finding for a key that was never read - a
                 confident status that is not evidence. The not_equals "*" direction is
                 correct on all three listeners: "*" is the documented default and "matches
                 anything", while a real restriction such as "!192.0.2/24, *" is not equal
                 to "*" and passes, which is the behavior the specification's own example
                 describes (web.conf.spec:1090-1096, inputs.conf.spec:587-591).
finding:         Splunk accepts inbound connections without a zone restriction on at least
                 one listener it owns: web.conf [settings] acceptFrom, server.conf
                 [httpServer] acceptFrom, or acceptFrom on the receiving input stanza is
                 unset or left at "*", which accepts connections from anywhere.
